        (212, 'Fig. 5.2', 9.0, 'CMUSerif-BoldSlanted', True, 2301728, 279.0, 161.3, 313.8, 173.2),
        (212, 'Can two straight lines intersect at more than one point?', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 185.1, 415.9, 202.3),
        (212, 'Activity 1', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Bold', True, 2301728, 92.4, 217.3, 150.8, 233.4),
        (212, 'Draw two lines on a plain sheet of paper so that they intersect. Measure', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 231.9, 503.5, 249.1),
        (212, 'the four angles formed with a protractor. Draw four such pairs of', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 246.3, 503.5, 263.5),
        (212, 'intersecting lines and measure the angles formed at the points of', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 260.7, 503.5, 277.9),
        (212, 'intersection.', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 275.1, 165.2, 292.3),
        (212, 'What patterns do you observe among these angles?', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 298.5, 391.1, 315.7),
        (212, 'In Fig. 5.2, if ∠a is 120°, can you figure out the measurements of ∠b, ∠c', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 330.9, 500.4, 348.1),
        (212, 'and ∠d, without drawing and measuring them?', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 345.3, 368.3, 362.5),
        (212, 'We know that ∠a and ∠b together measure 180°, because when they', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 106.8, 368.7, 503.5, 385.9),
        (212, 'are combined, they form a straight angle which measures 180°. So, if', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 383.1, 503.5, 400.3),
        (212, '∠a is 120°, then ∠b must be 60°.', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 397.5, 276.9, 414.7),
        (212, 'Similarly, ∠b and ∠c together measure 180°. So, if ∠b is 60°, then ∠c', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 106.8, 411.9, 500.4, 429.1),
        (212, 'must be 120°. And ∠c and ∠d together measure 180°. So, if ∠c is 120°,', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 426.3, 503.5, 443.5),
        (212, 'then ∠d must be 60°.', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 440.7, 212.9, 457.9),
        (212, 'Therefore, in Fig. 5.2, ∠a and ∠c measure 120°, and ∠b and ∠d', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 106.8, 455.1, 500.4, 472.3),
        (212, 'measure 60°.', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 469.5, 167.7, 486.7),
        (212, 'When two lines intersect each other and form four angles, labelled', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 106.8, 483.9, 503.5, 501.1),
        (212, 'a, b, c and d, as in Fig. 5.2, then ∠a and ∠c are equal, and ∠b and ∠d', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 498.3, 500.4, 515.5),
        (212, 'are equal!', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 512.7, 151.3, 529.9),
        (212, 'Is this always true for any pair of intersecting lines?', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 536.1, 395.0, 553.3),
        (212, 'Check this for different measures of ∠a. Using these measurements,', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 106.8, 559.5, 503.5, 576.7),
        (212, 'can you reason whether this property holds true for any measure of ∠a?', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 573.9, 500.4, 591.1),
        (212, 'We can generalise our reasoning for Fig. 5.2, without assuming the', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 106.8, 588.3, 503.5, 605.5),
        (212, 'values of ∠a.', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 602.7, 167.5, 619.9),
        (212, '\x07Since straight angles measure 180°, we must have ∠a + ∠b = ∠a + ∠d', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 106.8, 617.1, 500.4, 634.3),
        (212, '= 180°. Hence, ∠b and ∠d are always equal. Similarly, ∠b + ∠a = ∠b +', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 106.8, 631.5, 503.5, 648.7),
        (212, '∠c = 180°, so ∠a and ∠c must be equal.', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 106.8, 645.9, 338.2, 663.1),
        (212, 'Adjacent angles, like ∠a and ∠b, formed by two lines intersecting', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 106.8, 660.3, 503.6, 677.5),
        (212, 'each other, are called linear pairs. Linear pairs always add up to 180°.', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 674.5, 500.4, 691.9),
        (212, '107', 10.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 481.6, 735.2, 498.6, 749.6),
        (212, 'Reprint 2026-27', 8.0, 'Arial', False, 0, 267.7, 760.8, 325.1, 771.7),
        (213, 'Ganita Prakash | Grade 7', 10.0, 'NotoSerif-Italic', False, 2301728, 92.4, 35.8, 217.4, 50.2),
        (213, 'Opposite angles, like ∠b and ∠d, formed by two lines intersecting', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 106.8, 68.1, 503.5, 85.3),
        (213, 'each other, are called vertically opposite angles. Vertically opposite', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 82.2, 503.5, 99.7),
        (213, 'angles are always equal to each other.', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 96.9, 312.4, 114.1),
        (213, 'From the above reasoning, we conclude that whenever two lines', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 106.8, 111.3, 503.5, 128.5),
        (213, 'intersect, vertically opposite angles are equal. Such a justification is', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 125.7, 503.5, 142.9),
        (213, 'called a proof in mathematics.', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 139.8, 271.0, 157.3),
        (213, 'Figure it Out', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Bold', True, 12272689, 92.4, 163.2, 171.0, 179.4),
        (213, 'List all the linear pairs and vertically opposite angles you', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 177.9, 426.5, 195.1),
        (213, 'observe in Fig. 5.3:', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 192.3, 201.0, 209.5),
        (213, 'Linear Pairs', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 118.9, 220.4, 190.1, 237.7),
        (213, '∠a and ∠b, …', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 225.1, 220.4, 303.1, 237.7),
        (213, 'Pairs of Vertically', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 103.0, 246.4, 209.2, 263.7),
        (213, 'Opposite Angles', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 107.8, 260.8, 201.2, 278.1),
        (213, '∠b and ∠d, …', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 225.1, 253.6, 303.1, 270.9),
        (213, 'Measurements and Geometry', 14.0, 'NotoSerif-Bold', True, 1268080, 92.4, 302.0, 304.9, 320.8),
        (213, 'You might have noticed that when you measure linear pairs, sometimes', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 327.8, 503.5, 345.0),
        (213, 'they may not add up to 180°. Or, when you measure vertically opposite', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 342.2, 503.5, 359.4),
        (213, 'angles they may be unequal sometimes. What are the reasons for this?', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 356.6, 500.4, 373.8),
        (213, 'There could be different reasons:', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 371.0, 285.3, 388.2),
        (213, '• Measurement errors because of improper use of measuring', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 106.4, 389.9, 503.5, 407.1),
        (213, 'instruments — in this case, a protractor', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 122.4, 404.3, 351.1, 421.5),
        (213, '• Variation in the thickness of the lines drawn. The “ideal” line in', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 106.4, 423.2, 503.5, 440.4),
        (213, 'geometry does not have any thickness! But it is not possible for us', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 122.4, 437.6, 503.5, 454.8),
        (213, 'to draw lines without any thickness', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 122.4, 452.0, 329.7, 469.2),
        (213, 'In geometry, we create ideal versions of “lines” and other shapes', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 106.8, 466.4, 503.5, 483.6),
        (213, 'we see around us, and analyse the relationships between them. For', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 480.8, 503.5, 498.0),
        (213, 'example, we know that the angle formed by a straight line is 180°.', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 495.2, 503.5, 512.4),
        (213, 'So, if another line divides this angle into two parts, both parts should', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 509.6, 503.5, 526.8),
        (213, 'add up to 180°. We arrive at this simply through reasoning and not', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 524.0, 503.5, 541.2),
        (213, 'by measurement. When we measure, it might not be exactly so, for', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 538.4, 503.5, 555.6),
        (213, 'the reasons mentioned above. Still the measurements come out very', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 552.8, 503.5, 570.0),
        (213, 'close to what we predict, because of which geometry finds widespread', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 567.2, 503.5, 584.4),
        (213, 'application in different disciplines such as physics, art, engineering', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 581.6, 503.5, 598.8),
        (213, 'and architecture.', 12.0, 'NotoSerif-Regular', False, 2301728, 92.4, 596.0, 192.5, 613.2),
